\description{
Performs k-fold or leave-future-out cross validation with sdmTMB models.
Returns the sum of log likelihoods of held-out data (log predictive density),
which can be used to compare models—higher values indicate better
out-of-sample prediction. By default, creates folds randomly and stratified
by time (set a seed for reproducibility), but folds can be manually assigned
via \code{fold_ids}. See Ward and Anderson (2025) in the References and the
\href{https://sdmTMB.github.io/sdmTMB/articles/cross-validation.html}{cross-validation vignette}.
}
\details{
\strong{Plug-in vs. integrated predictive densities}

With \code{predictive = "plugin"}, held-out observations are scored at the
conditional mode of the random effects, \eqn{\log p(y \mid \hat{u},
\hat{\theta})}, which treats the random fields as known. With
\code{"random"}, the score is \eqn{\log E[p(y \mid u, \hat{\theta})]} over
\eqn{u \sim N(\hat{u}, H^{-1})}, where \eqn{H} is the inner (Laplace)
Hessian of the training fit. With \code{"joint"}, fixed effects and variance
parameters (on their unconstrained scale) are also drawn from the joint
precision matrix. Each is averaged over \code{nsim} draws per fold separately
for each held-out observation, so \code{fold_loglik} is
\eqn{\sum_i \log E[p(y_i \mid u)]}. If
\code{reml = TRUE}, fixed effects are treated as random effects and are
therefore also drawn under \code{"random"}. For Gaussian observations,
\code{"random"} is exact given \eqn{\hat{\theta}}; for other families it
relies on the Laplace approximation's Gaussian approximation to the
random effects' posterior.

\strong{Parallel processing}

Parallel processing can be used by setting a \code{future::plan()}.

For example:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{library(future)
plan(multisession)
# now use sdmTMB_cv() ...
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Leave-future-out cross validation (LFOCV)}

An example of LFOCV with 9 time steps, \code{lfo_forecast = 1}, and
\code{lfo_validations = 2}:
\itemize{
\item Fit data to time steps 1 to 7, predict and validate step 8.
\item Fit data to time steps 1 to 8, predict and validate step 9.
}

An example of LFOCV with 9 time steps, \code{lfo_forecast = 2}, and
\code{lfo_validations = 3}:
\itemize{
\item Fit data to time steps 1 to 5, predict and validate step 7.
\item Fit data to time steps 1 to 6, predict and validate step 8.
\item Fit data to time steps 1 to 7, predict and validate step 9.
}

Note that these are time steps in the order they appear in the data.
For example, in the \code{pcod} data example below, steps between observations
are not always one year, so \code{lfo_forecast = 2} forecasts two observed time
steps ahead, not necessarily two calendar years.

See example below.
}
